1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,740
Okay, next section, salivary gland.

2
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:05,360
Remember that the salivary glands are exocrine glands that secrete saliva.

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:09,140
They are divided into the major glands and the minor glands.

4
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:11,300
And remember that the major glands are three.

5
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:15,300
There is a parotid gland, a submandibular gland, and a sublingual gland.

6
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:19,880
And there are thousands of minor glands scattered throughout the oral pharyngeal mucosa.

7
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:22,800
The first disorder that we want to talk about is mumps,

8
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:24,880
and this is when you get infection with the mumps virus.

9
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:29,040
Remember that classically the mumps virus infects the parotid glands,

10
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:32,180
giving you bilateral inflamed parotid glands.

11
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:35,840
The mumps virus can also infect the testicle, giving you orchitis.

12
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,980
And again, remember that the feared complication of mumps

13
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:41,960
is that the patients can develop sterility.

14
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:45,440
And so orchitis would carry the risk of sterility.

15
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:48,320
Of course, that risk would only be seen in teenagers

16
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,020
because patients less than the age of 10 usually don't get orchitis,

17
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:54,820
whereas patients greater than the age of 10 do.

18
00:00:54,820 --> 00:01:00,760
And that's why we particularly worry about orchitis occurring in a teenager

19
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:03,140
resulting in a risk of sterility.

20
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:07,560
Now, it's also possible for the mumps virus to create pancreatitis,

21
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:12,020
and that's important because remember that when you get pancreatitis,

22
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:13,800
you're going to get an elevated serum amylase,

23
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:16,060
but the serum amylase will also be elevated

24
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:18,320
due to the fact that you have salivary gland involvement.

25
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:21,720
So when you have an elevated serum amylase in a patient with mumps,

26
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:25,440
it could be due to the salivary gland because the salivary gland produces amylase,

27
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:29,840
or it could be due to the pancreatitis because in pancreatitis you also get

28
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:32,020
an elevated serum amylase.

29
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:36,860
And then the fourth place where the infection can occur is within the meninges,

30
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:40,360
and so you can get something called an aseptic meningitis,

31
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:45,280
and that can also be present in patients who have a mumps virus infection.

32
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:49,360
Psyladenitis is just inflammation of the salivary gland.

33
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:50,780
There really isn't much to say here.

34
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:55,060
What happens is that the patients develop an obstructing stone.

35
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:59,700
That's called sialolithiasis, and remember the general principle in pathology.

36
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:03,020
Anytime you block a tube, you're going to get infection behind that block.

37
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:07,140
And so when the stone obstructs, you end up getting inflammation of the salivary gland.

38
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:12,400
It's usually due to a staph aureus infection, and it's usually unilateral as well.

39
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:13,780
So that's psialatinitis.

40
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:17,640
One of the important tumors of the salivary gland to be familiar with,

41
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:20,700
and the most common tumor is a pleomorphic adenoma.

42
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:24,960
The word pleomorphic here implies that you've got multiple types of tissue.

43
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:30,560
And so this is a benign tumor composed of both stromal tissue and epithelial tissue.

44
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:36,160
It's important to note that when you've got two types of tissue, i.e. stroma and epithelium,

45
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:38,480
we often call that a biphasic tumor.

46
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:40,200
So it's a biphasic tumor.

47
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:43,240
Now the stromal component can be cartilage, for example,

48
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:46,460
and the epithelial component may have glands within it.

49
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:50,800
And so this is what we would classically see within a pleomorphic adenoma.

50
00:02:51,260 --> 00:02:53,200
Now it's important to note that, again, as I've stated,

51
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:57,420
it's the most common tumor of the salivary gland, usually arises in the parotid.

52
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:04,960
And it classically presents as a mobile, painless, circumscribed mass at the region of the parotid gland.

53
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:11,140
Now this is very important because these are all of the key characteristics that we've studied concerning benign tumors.

54
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:14,380
It's mobile, meaning that it hasn't invaded local tissue.

55
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:18,980
It's painless because it hasn't invaded the facial nerve.

56
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:23,420
Now remember that the facial nerve runs right through the parotid gland.

57
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:27,440
And so when you have malignant tumors of the parotid gland,

58
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:31,600
you often have facial nerve problems, and pain may be one of them.

59
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:36,020
Also, important to note that this is a circumscribed mass,

60
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:40,340
so that the tumor is very distinct from all of the tissue surrounding it.

61
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:43,900
And all of these features are suggested that we're dealing with a benign tumor.

62
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:48,280
Now important to note, and very high yield for the purposes of examinations,

63
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:50,480
is that there's a high rate of recurrence in this tumor.

64
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:55,800
The reason why you get a high rate of recurrence in this tumor is because the tumor has irregular margins.

65
00:03:56,180 --> 00:04:01,140
So if you look at this picture here, what I'm outlining here is a pleomorphic adenoma.

66
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:06,140
And what you can see is that there is some irregularity to the margins of this tumor.

67
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:09,820
So imagine if the surgeon goes in and excises this tumor,

68
00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:14,380
but leaves behind a little bit of that irregularity, that can result in recurrence.

69
00:04:14,380 --> 00:04:18,900
And so the reason why there is a high rate of recurrence is because surgeons will often,

70
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:25,960
or classically, an inexperienced surgeon, we should say, may not completely resect the entire tumor.

71
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:30,300
So that leads to the high rate of recurrence, as I've stated, and again, that's super high yield.

72
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:34,380
Rarely, this pleomorphic adenoma can transform into carcinoma.

73
00:04:34,380 --> 00:04:39,440
And the way by which you'll make that distinction is that you'll get signs of facial nerve damage,

74
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:41,380
for example, facial nerve palsies.

75
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:46,260
And so the classic example that might show up on an examination would be a patient

76
00:04:46,260 --> 00:04:48,920
who has a mass in the parotid for 10 years,

77
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:53,100
and then all of a sudden now presents with some sort of facial nerve abnormality.

78
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:56,520
So if a patient has a mass in a parotid for 10 years that isn't growing,

79
00:04:56,640 --> 00:04:59,180
that suggests that you have like a pleomorphic adenoma.

80
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:02,300
And then all of a sudden, they now present with facial nerve problems.

81
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:05,940
That suggests that the tumor is now boring into the facial nerve.

82
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:09,840
And so you'd think about transformation into carcinoma.

83
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:12,500
The next tumor is called a warthens tumor.

84
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:16,460
This is a benign cystic tumor of the salivary gland.

85
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:19,280
It's the second most common salivary gland tumor.

86
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:21,520
Almost always arises in the parotid.

87
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:26,920
Now this benign cystic tumor, the classic feature is that you get abundant lymphocytes and germinal centers.

88
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:30,940
So basically, it's a cystic tumor with lymph node tissue.

89
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:32,160
That's the take-home point.

90
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:35,200
This tumor almost always arises in the parotid.

91
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:39,920
And the reason why it classically arises in the parotid is because it's not uncommon embryologically

92
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:44,160
for lymph node tissue to be associated with the parotid.

93
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:50,960
The parotid gland is one of the last glands to actually separate itself from the adjacent stroma embryologically.

94
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:54,860
And so it's often that we can see lymph node tissue within the parotid,

95
00:05:54,860 --> 00:05:57,940
and that lymph node tissue can actually become a warthens tumor.

96
00:05:58,320 --> 00:06:01,540
So again, it's a benign cystic tumor with lymph node tissue.

97
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:06,120
And that's why we say abundant lymphocytes and germinal centers is the classic description.

98
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:09,620
The final tumor is called a mucoepidermoid carcinoma.

99
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:14,020
And the muco means that you have mucus-producing cells or mucinous cells.

100
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:16,880
And the epidermoid means that you've got squamous cells.

101
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:20,620
So it's actually a malignant tumor that's composed of two types of epithelium,

102
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,620
mucinous and squamous cells.

103
00:06:22,620 --> 00:06:27,380
Now, this usually arises in the parotid, and it's going to commonly involve the facial nerve.

104
00:06:27,460 --> 00:06:28,320
It's a malignant tumor.

105
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:31,120
So, again, the facial nerve runs right through the parotid.

106
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:34,480
And if you have a malignant tumor of the parotid, it'll damage the facial nerve,

107
00:06:34,580 --> 00:06:36,720
creating problems with the facial nerve.

108
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:39,540
And so that's important to bear in mind as well.

109
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,080
And that concludes our discussion of salivary gland lesions.

110
00:06:44,080 --> 00:07:14,060
Salivary gland lesions.

